AD9361 native converter support
===============================

Origin: Analog Devices no-OS, commit
ce7107f957d19858f2dffc85bf3d7f2c70367000
https://github.com/analogdevicesinc/no-OS/tree/ce7107f957d19858f2dffc85bf3d7f2c70367000/drivers/rf-transceiver/ad9361

The imported AD9361 core, API, utility declarations and external band control
retain their BSD-3-Clause notices. LICENSE_BSD accompanies the import. New
native transport, FDT, converter and receive-tuning code carries BSD-2-Clause
notices. The FDT defaults retain the original 2013 Analog Devices copyright,
author and BSD-3-Clause notice from that revision's AD9361 example;
board-specific settings remain in the board's device tree.

The native attachment uses the kernel SPI and FDT GPIO/clock interfaces. It
performs only a product-ID probe and raw status reads during RX attachment.
TX attachment keeps GPIO reset asserted and defers all radio SPI access until
an exclusive owner requests bootstrap.
Disruptive work requires the generic ADI converter's exclusive capture lease
and explicit adi,rx-only opt-in. This implementation accepts two receive
channels in FDD mode, internal receive LO, and SPI-controlled ENSM. External
TX LO, independent FDD and ENSM pin control are rejected.

Bootstrap programs RX clocks and analog calibration without ADC register
access. The capture owner must subsequently prove actual quiescence; cold
admission is not evidence of drained AXI transactions. Normal configuration
changes RX frequency, sample rate, bandwidth and gain, then performs a new
receive PN9 eye search at that sample rate through the ADC owner's callbacks.
There is no rate-independent calibration cache.

RX-only adaptations clear both TX channel-enable bits, apply synthesizer
power-down overrides and attenuation, and skip TX analog/filter/quadrature
calibrations and TX digital tuning. The transport validates every descending
multi-register write before sending it, prohibits unsafe transmitter and
external-control values, and freshly reads the TX inhibit registers before
ENSM writes. A transport error is sticky: no subsequent SPI transaction is
issued, configuration fails and RESETB is asserted with physical pin readback.
These checks describe register state; they are not an RF emission measurement.

Native adaptations also provide kmem allocation and delay callbacks, use
native errno values, prefix clock/arithmetic compatibility helpers, reject
the userland FIR text parser, and return EOPNOTSUPP from the unused legacy
AXI tuning bridge. Typed FIR APIs remain imported but are not exposed by the
native converter interface. Checked clock/calibration readbacks prevent
invalid shifts, zero divisors and zero-duration RSSI indexing after transport
failure. Synthesizer and clock setter errors propagate before cache updates.

An independent TX provider requires adi,tx-enable and rejects adi,rx-only.
It accepts internal-LO FDD, SPI-controlled ENSM and 2R2T, with a fixed native
13 MSPS / 2 MHz bootstrap profile. The owner must arm an independent hardware
reset deadline and zero the DAC before releasing GPIO reset. Bootstrap performs
the normal no-OS reset and TX calibration at maximum attenuation. TX2 remains
at 89750 mdB; TX1 attenuation and retuning require the same exclusive owner.
Operational errors assert RESETB and invalidate the lease. Fresh status reads
verify clocks, PLL locks, attenuation and both quadrature calibration results.

Native TX explicitly uses the device-tree delay taps and bypasses the unused
legacy AXI bridge. This is not a successful digital eye search:
digital_tuning_valid remains false, and physical interface qualification is
required. The generic provider has no application protocol, user identity,
RF policy or private FPGA dependency. See sys/dev/spi/ad9361var.h for owner
lifetime and reset ordering, and ember/tools/tests/ad9361/README.md for tests.

Build integration is in sys/dev/spi/files.spi under ad9361phy. The generic
ADC/converter and DMA providers are separate drivers.

Host regression, from the source root:

  AD9361_SANITIZE=1 sh ember/tools/tests/ad9361/native-test.sh "$PWD"

The regression compiles the actual imported code with small kernel API shims,
uses generic synthetic FDT properties, runs staged bootstrap/configure, and
injects each bootstrap SPI failure plus invalid calibration/PLL status. Its
register backend supplies synthetic lock, ENSM and PN acknowledgements; it
does not qualify the radio, DMA, ADC clock wiring or physical PN timing.
